"comparison": "Both disclosures concern the presentation and accounting of interest income and expense related to prime brokerage customer balances. Goldman Sachs (GS) discloses that customer payables, which primarily consist of customer credit balances from prime brokerage activities, are accounted for at cost plus accrued interest. This directly relates to Morgan Stanley's (MS) topic of offsetting interest income and expense for prime brokerage-related customer receivables and payables to align balance sheet treatment with interest recognition. While MS describes a retrospective presentation change adjusting both sides of the offset by $4,432 million, GS describes the standing accounting method for similar prime brokerage payables without noting a recent presentation change.",
